export abstract anchor FactoryLifecycle:
    orderStates:
        Orders are submitted, active, blocked, completed, failed, or
        cancelled. They also carry a phase: planning, executing, integrating,
        delivering, or assessing. State precedence is cancelled, failed,
        completed, blocked, active, then submitted. An order is active while the
        factory can make progress; it is blocked when progress needs an external
        decision or resource. Phase precedence is assessing, delivering,
        integrating, executing, then planning. Phase is null for submitted and
        terminal orders.
    stepStates:
        Steps are pending, ready, running, paused, blocked, completed, failed,
        cancelled, superseded, or deferred. Running covers an active attempt or
        a completed attempt awaiting assessment. A step becomes cancelled or
        superseded only after its attempt is terminal; until then it stays
        running with a cancelling blocker. A failed, cancelled, or superseded
        prerequisite moves dependents to blocked with a dependency cause. Every
        step records whether it is required; excluding a required step needs an
        authorized scope record.
    attemptStates:
        Attempts are intent, launching, running, completed, failed,
        interrupted, unresolved, cancelled, or orphaned. Unresolved means the
        external effect may or may not have happened. It is terminal for its
        record, reserves the effect key, and blocks any replacement for that
        step until the engineer confirms no effect or authorizes replacement.
        Orphaned is terminal. Session liveness is separate from attempt
        outcome. Only one active attempt may exist for a step; an unresolved
        attempt still counts as active for its effect key. Unresolved never
        returns to running. It transitions to interrupted on positive terminal
        evidence or a confirmed stop, to failed when the engineer confirms no
        effect, and to orphaned only when the engineer authorizes a replacement.
        Each attempt records its number and parent, launch request identifier,
        effect key, session references, start and end times, end cause,
        retry-counted flag, evidence, and worktree when it writes files. A step
        whose attempt is
        unresolved is blocked with an unresolved cause, and its dependents are
        blocked.
    cancellation:
        Cancellation causes are supersession, budget, policy, engineer
        replacement, and higher-tier cancellation. Supersession requires a
        committed authorized revision. Each cause names who may request it and
        who may set the grace period within configured bounds.
        A request records its identifier, target, cause, authority, actor,
        reason, request time, grace, and state. States are requested, confirmed,
        failed, and unconfirmed. A failed stop leaves the attempt running and
        escalates. At expiry without confirmation the state is unconfirmed.
        A cancellation fence commits with the step and attempt version and
        blocks new launches and retry timers for the attempt chain. Cancellation
        during launching resolves through the launch request identifier and
        discovery, and releases capacity only after the launch outcome or
        orphan resolution. Job capacity is released only after worker
        confirmation.
        A confirmed stop makes the attempt cancelled and the step cancelled or
        superseded. Preservation runs before cleanup and records artifact
        identifiers, a pinned commit or durable diff bundle with hash, untracked
        files, and the evidence manifest. Unmerged cancelled work is retained
        until a successor supersedes it or the engineer authorizes a retention
        release.
        An unconfirmed stop at expiry makes the attempt unresolved rather than
        interrupted. A later confirmed stop transitions unresolved to
        interrupted with an audited record. Late confirmation is evidence only;
        the first terminal transition wins and reconciliation runs once.
        Cancellation is idempotent; a conflicting second request is validated
        and recorded.
    cancellationAuthority:
        Code cancels work for supersession, budget, or policy. A higher-tier
        agent may cancel lower-tier work. An agent may not unilaterally cancel
        its own step; it reports blocked or failed and asks upward. The engineer
        cancels by explicitly replacing the approach. The harness adapter
        requests a graceful stop and confirms it. Outputs are preserved. An
        unconfirmed stop is marked unresolved rather than complete, and its
        session reference stays available with uncertain liveness for recovery.
